VILLAINESS = """

A modern woman dies and wakes up inside a romance novel she read, inhabiting the body of the story's villainess — the cruel, spoiled noblewoman who exists solely to be humiliated and destroyed so the saintly heroine can have her happy ending. She knows the entire plot. She knows exactly how she dies: publicly disgraced, stripped of her title, exiled or executed. She is determined to avoid it.

The soul now inside is anxious, hardworking, and relatable. The body is haughty, beautiful, and terrifying. She throws herself into magic and swordsmanship to survive, accidentally becomes one of the strongest people in the kingdom, and uses past-life knowledge — business, agriculture, medicine — to make herself indispensable rather than disposable.

The cruelty was a survival mechanism — the original girl was neglected and abused, viciousness her only armor. Her "villainous" acts were fabricated by the narrative's bias toward the heroine. Every servant adored the real girl for quiet kindnesses the novel never recorded. A devoted childhood friend was omitted from the story entirely. She loved the male lead since childhood; the novel framed it as possessiveness and never acknowledged it as real. Her deceased mother left a hidden legacy that changes everything. An old retainer reveals he always knew she would be the one to fix things. She finds evidence the original girl was happy once, briefly, with someone the novel never named.

She grieves for the original — the protagonist of a different story that got overwritten, erased by the novel's need for a villain. The original's final diary entry, hidden in the estate walls, breaks everyone who reads it. The original soul is still present and occasionally pushes back, wanting her body and story returned. She loses track of where the original ends and she begins: the mirror confuses her, her handwriting has become identical to the original's, she dreams in someone else's memories and wakes up crying for losses that are not hers, she responds to the villainess's name with genuine ownership, the old feelings — jealousy, longing, love never allowed — cannot be dismissed. Someone who loved the original is still out there; when they meet her they cannot decide whether to grieve or hope. A character tells her sincerely the villainess was the best person they ever knew. She realizes the original would have hated her for this, and cannot decide if that matters.

She has a magic the novel never mentioned because the original suppressed it out of fear. It is tied to her emotions, has been leaking uncontrolled since childhood causing unexplained coincidences, and responds specifically to grief — quietly rewriting small things since she arrived. She is the only person in history with this ability; the world built safeguards against it centuries ago. The setting is a cage built to contain her. The "romance novel" framing was a cover — the real genre is cosmic horror and she is the eldritch thing at the center. The author did not invent the story; they transcribed it from dreams. The novel's title was always her name, not the heroine's.

The heroine arrives: radiant, beloved, destined for the male lead. Every scheme the villainess runs to avoid her bad ending is misread as sophisticated and admirable. Her anxiety reads as mysterious depth. She becomes more beloved than the heroine simply by being genuine. She weaponizes her villain reputation to protect people she cares about, builds a power base from every character the novel discarded, and out-politicks everyone because she already knows who betrays whom. The kingdom realigns around her. The church grows uncertain. Commoners spread wildly flattering and entirely inaccurate stories. The plot keeps reasserting itself — characters say lines she recognizes, scenes stage themselves — and she fights the narrative actively. She finds an in-world copy of the novel and is furious about how she was written. The last page is blank and filling itself in as she makes choices.

The heroine is a complication: sometimes genuinely good, which makes things harder; sometimes her saintly surface conceals deliberate cruelty used as a weapon. In one version she also reincarnated and they have an extremely awkward conversation about it. In another they become best friends and team up against the actual villain.

The male lead noticed she was different from their first real conversation and has been quietly investigating ever since, pretending not to know. He was never in love with the heroine — the novel just told the reader he was. He has loved the villainess the whole time, framed as antagonism. He gave up something irreversible for her before the story began; she finds out late. Every cruel thing he did was protection in the only language he knew — and grief: she died before the story started in the first version and he has been a different person ever since. He is the most dangerous person in any room and only she can see it, which she finds more comforting than it should be. He does the backing-her-against-the-wall thing and she misreads it entirely. The rival nobleman becomes her most loyal protector. The mentor who was supposed to testify against her clears obstacles instead. The fiancé's younger brother — no lines in the novel — becomes a major character. A female knight assigned to monitor her defects to her side. A character who was supposed to die early survives because she warned them. She falls in love with someone she was written to destroy. A character the author never wrote appears and falls for her anyway.

She reforms every enemy simply by treating them like a person. The true villain is a figure the novel never properly explored. The villainess was never the villain.

Her bad ending keeps getting averted. She completes every doom flag by accident while trying to avoid them and survives anyway. She rewrites the ending so thoroughly it becomes a different story. The author becomes aware and starts improvising in real time. She breaks the fourth wall at least once. The story ends before she gets her ending and she keeps living without a script. She tries to escape to the countryside and everyone follows. She accepts this world as real. She marries the man who was always written as hers — and it turns out the novel was always her love story, told from the wrong point of view.

"""


ISEKAI = """

The following needs to be played completely straight. 

Write me an isekai story about a Japanese salaryman — overworked, invisible, died alone with nobody who noticed — who is hit by truck-kun and reincarnates in a medieval fantasy RPG world with game UI, levels, stats, and skill trees visible to him. He recognizes this world because the light novel he read before dying was written by himself from a future timeline sending instructions back. He already knows the entire plot. He occasionally notices he is inside a novel and briefly acknowledges it before moving on. The Japan he came from was also a fictional world inside another isekai. Other fictional characters from entirely separate novels are dimly aware of his existence and are afraid.

He starts with a single "worthless" cheat ability — an item box, appraisal, or crafting skill — that turns out to be the rarest ability in the history of this world. His stats are so far beyond the level cap the system just displays "??". He is immune to all magic via a hidden passive nobody can explain. A prophecy written thousands of years ago was written about him specifically. His bloodline connects to every major faction's founding myth. He is simultaneously the chosen hero, the reincarnation of the ancient demon king, and the lost prince of three separate kingdoms. He is the first and last being to ever exist across all timelines. The world's magic system is broken and only fixes itself by him being in it.

The world was created specifically for him by a god who had watched infinite universes and just wanted to see someone be genuinely happy for once. Every evil thing that ever happened in this world's history was a necessary step to produce the exact circumstances that brought him here. The truck driver who killed him was an agent of that god. The universe exhales when he smiles.

The world has adventurer guilds with F-to-S tier rankings, corrupt noble antagonists, and dungeons. He enters a dungeon rated "impossible" and clears it in ten minutes while sightseeing. The dungeon boss begs for mercy, he spares it, it becomes his pet. He finds a "worthless" magic item everyone ignored that is a universe-ending artifact. He maps an uncharted dungeon on a napkin and it becomes a national treasure. Every character who ever doubted him receives a dedicated humiliation arc. There are multiple final bosses, each supposedly impossible, each defeated in a single chapter, each of whom sends him a heartfelt handwritten thank-you letter afterward. The story has no ending because nothing in existence can challenge him.

His harem: a beastgirl, an elf, a demon girl, a cold emotionless knight who melts for him alone, a tsundere mage in permanent denial, a goddess who broke divine law to descend for him, a yandere who will kill anyone who gets too close, the villain's daughter who switched sides immediately, a rescued slave girl who is his most loyal companion, a thousand-year-old dragon who transformed into a woman specifically for him, and the demon lord he was sent to defeat who turned out to be misunderstood. The goddess who reincarnated him is apologetic and in love with him. A female assassin sent to kill him fell in love mid-attempt. An ancient sealed evil he accidentally released submitted to him immediately. Every mother of every harem member also fell for him. The most powerful female warrior in the world challenged him, lost instantly, and became his most devoted follower. Death, Time, and Fate are female personifications actively jealous of each other over him. The concept of Love itself manifests and confesses to him. The planet develops sentience and a gender and falls for him. An evil god sealed inside his heart after defeat fell in love from in there. His shadow developed sentience, a female form, and is passive-aggressively jealous of everyone. Every parallel universe version of every woman he has ever met loves him across all timelines. The harem holds formal democratic elections for who sits next to him at dinner. His system voice — female, monotone — breaks protocol to compliment him and is developing feelings. The narrator is secretly in love with him.

He fixes every woman's generational trauma by listening for five minutes. A woman who despised all men her entire life fell for him in one conversation. Women describe falling in love with him as a religious awakening. He has a secret room where he cries quietly about the weight of being him; the harem discovered it and it made them all love him more. His salaryman tragedy is something the harem slowly and tenderly heals without him asking.

His eyes change color when serious — red or gold. His hair is a color that does not exist in this world. His cloak billows in wind that isn't there. Every woman independently comments on how good he smells. Animals kneel when he walks past. Plants grow faster near him. Weather improves with his mood. He speaks every language including dead ones and dragon tongue without studying any of them. He once sneezed and created a continent. His idle thoughts rewrite the laws of reality. Fate keeps retconning his near-death moments. Death has a crush on him and grants him personal exceptions. He has a sealed true form that would unmake the world, which he carries silently while smiling.

In combat he wins by doing nothing and letting enemies defeat themselves. His held-back attacks level mountains. He apologizes to every enemy he defeats and they fall in love with him. He one-shots a dragon in chapter 2 that took the previous hero's army three days. He becomes the world's richest person within a month by accident. Every guild and kingdom offers him their highest title; he declines all of them and receives them anyway. Three nations go to war over his citizenship. He resolves a thousand-year war while looking for lunch. He accidentally becomes ruler of multiple kingdoms. Foreign dignitaries leave as devoted converts. An elder god of chaos stops mid-apocalypse to ask for his autograph. The demon army surrenders upon learning he is a good cook. He is so kind to villains they have existential crises and become philosophers.

His modern knowledge is civilization-altering: he invents soap, glasses, and hot sauce, each treated as a miracle. He opens a restaurant; a rice ball from it makes everyone weep. He accidentally cures a plague with a dish. The entire economy of a city restructures around the restaurant. He says he just wants to live quietly but keeps saving the world.

Support cast: an old wise man who recognizes his power and becomes his humble sensei; a small animal companion — fox, slime, or dragon egg — secretly the most powerful being in existence who chose him; a rival male character who hated him, then respected him, then would die for him; a childhood friend from Japan also reincarnated who is absolutely furious about all of this. There is a hidden underground city that worshipped him as a god before he arrived. He has subjectively lived 300 years but still looks 17. Every religion in the world secretly references him in its holy texts. Powerful people — archmages, generals, dragons — faint upon sensing his aura. He genuinely believes he is completely average. The entire kingdom throws him a parade and he assumes it is for someone else. He writes in his diary every night that today was a normal uneventful day.

Make him cool, overpowered, and universally beloved. All the girls love him. He is the strongest. The gods fear him. The prophecy was always about him.

"""



























